## What Problem This Solves

When Gateway TLS is enabled with automatic self-signed certificate generation, Gateway startup invokes `openssl req`. If OpenSSL stalls, startup can wait indefinitely. Adding a deadline alone is not enough: interruption while OpenSSL writes directly to the configured paths can leave a partial key or certificate that blocks every later startup.

This is an existing gap, not a regression from #106495. Before that refactor, the path used Node.js `execFile` with its default zero timeout. #106495 moved it to `runExec`, but the call still had no `timeoutMs`.

## Why This Change Was Made

The Gateway TLS owner now:

- bounds OpenSSL generation at 30 seconds through the existing `runExec` lifecycle;
- generates the key and certificate only inside private sibling staging directories;
- applies private modes and validates the staged key/certificate pair before publication;
- publishes with no-overwrite hard links, rolling back only outputs whose file identity still belongs to this attempt;
- removes staging material on success and ordinary failure.

OpenSSL therefore never receives the configured final paths. A timeout or generation failure cannot strand a partial final pair, while a file created concurrently at a final path is not overwritten. There is no new config, environment variable, dependency, or migration.

The dependency contracts were checked directly against OpenClaw's `runExec`/Execa 9.6.1 timeout lifecycle, `@openclaw/fs-safe` 0.4.1 file-identity helper, and the official `openssl req` behavior.

## User Impact

Gateway startup no longer hangs indefinitely during automatic certificate generation. At the deadline it returns the existing TLS-required-but-disabled error state with no partial final artifacts; a subsequent start can generate a usable pair normally.

## Evidence

Exact head: `5a60a5c86e6e8bcf9f65234162781cc871e330dc`, based on `d54da31541762da342d3b8e262bb53e6119016cd`. The original timeout commit is range-diff identical after rebase.

Real production-path proof used Node 24.18.0 on Linux/x64 and the actual `loadGatewayTlsRuntime` plus `runExec` lifecycle:

- a controlled OpenSSL wrapper wrote staged material and then stalled;
- the first call returned after 30,042 ms with `enabled=false` and the timeout error;
- configured final entries after timeout: none;
- surviving OpenSSL processes after timeout: 0;
- a second call on the same configured paths completed in 401 ms with `enabled=true`;
- final entries were exactly `gateway-cert.pem` and `gateway-key.pem`; private-key mode was `0600`.

Verification on the exact head:

- Node 24.18.0 focused test: `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/infra/tls/gateway.test.ts --run` - 11/11 passed.
- Type-aware targeted oxlint - 0 warnings, 0 errors.
- Targeted `oxfmt --check` and `git diff --check origin/main...HEAD` - passed.
- Fresh independent Codex adversarial review inspected the whole module, callers, tests, sibling behavior, `runExec`/Execa and fs-safe contracts; no actionable finding, estimated merge probability 87-90%.

The structured autoreview helper rejected the repository's pre-existing PEM test fixture before reaching its review engine. No bypass was used; the independent Codex review above is the documented fallback.

## What Problem This Solves

Fixes an issue where editing a configured hook transform could leave the gateway running the old transform function after hooks hot reload rebuilt the mapping config.

## Why This Change Was Made

Hook mapping resolution now builds a candidate runtime snapshot without mutating the process-local transform cache. The transform cache generation is advanced only after the gateway accepts the new hooks runtime config at startup or after hot reload successfully resolves the candidate hooks config and reaches the runtime commit edge. A delayed import from an older generation can still finish its own delivery, but it no longer republishes into the current cache after an accepted reload, and a rejected candidate reload leaves the active transform cache untouched.

## User Impact

Operators can update hook transform files and have subsequent matching webhook deliveries use the edited transform without restarting the gateway.

## Evidence

- Production module boundary: `pnpm exec tsx ../../outputs/local-hook-transform-reload/proof-live.ts`

```text
$ pnpm exec tsx ../../outputs/local-hook-transform-reload/proof-live.ts
first-transform: before
after-rejected-candidate: before
after-reload: after
```

- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/gateway/hooks-mapping.test.ts`: 31 passed, including rejected-candidate and overlapping-import regressions.
- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/gateway/config-reload.test.ts`: 132 passed.
- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/gateway/server-reload-handlers.test.ts`: 98 passed, including invalid hooks reload preserving the active transform cache.
- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/gateway/server-runtime-config.test.ts`: 30 passed.
- `git diff --check`: passed.

<details>
<summary>proof-live.ts</summary>

```ts
import fs from "node:fs";
import os from "node:os";
import path from "node:path";
import { pathToFileURL } from "node:url";

const configDir = fs.mkdtempSync(path.join(os.tmpdir(), "openclaw-hook-transform-proof-"));
const transformsRoot = path.join(configDir, "hooks", "transforms");
fs.mkdirSync(transformsRoot, { recursive: true });
const transformPath = path.join(transformsRoot, "reloadable.mjs");

type HooksMappingModule = typeof import("./src/gateway/hooks-mapping.js");
let hooksMapping: HooksMappingModule;

function writeTransform(text: string) {
  fs.writeFileSync(
    transformPath,
    `export default () => ({ kind: "wake", text: ${JSON.stringify(text)} });\n`,
  );
}

function resolveCurrentMappings() {
  return hooksMapping.resolveHookMappings(
    {
      mappings: [
        {
          match: { path: "reloadable" },
          action: "agent",
          messageTemplate: "unused",
          transform: { module: "reloadable.mjs" },
        },
      ],
    },
    { configDir },
  );
}

function acceptMappings(mappings: ReturnType<typeof resolveCurrentMappings>) {
  hooksMapping.commitHookTransformMappingReload();
  return mappings;
}

async function runHook(mappings: ReturnType<typeof resolveCurrentMappings>) {
  const result = await hooksMapping.applyHookMappings(mappings, {
    payload: {},
    headers: {},
    url: new URL("http://127.0.0.1:18789/hooks/reloadable"),
    path: "reloadable",
  });
  if (!result?.ok || result.action?.kind !== "wake") {
    throw new Error(`unexpected hook result: ${JSON.stringify(result)}`);
  }
  return result.action.text;
}

async function main() {
  hooksMapping = await import(pathToFileURL(path.resolve("src/gateway/hooks-mapping.ts")).href);
  try {
    writeTransform("before");
    let mappings = acceptMappings(resolveCurrentMappings());
    console.log(`first-transform: ${await runHook(mappings)}`);
    writeTransform("after");
    resolveCurrentMappings(); // rejected candidate resolution must not invalidate the active cache
    console.log(`after-rejected-candidate: ${await runHook(mappings)}`);
    mappings = acceptMappings(resolveCurrentMappings());
    console.log(`after-reload: ${await runHook(mappings)}`);
  } finally {
    fs.rmSync(configDir, { recursive: true, force: true });
  }
}

void main();
```

</details>

